<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Work hydration principles">
  <div><strong>Access first</strong><span>Cool potable water should be easy to reach throughout the shift.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Heat needs controls</strong><span>Rest, cooling, acclimatization and scheduling matter alongside drinking.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Electrolytes are situational</strong><span>They become more relevant with prolonged sweating, not simply because a shift is long.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Shift design</p>
    <h2>Do not make hydration depend on remembering too late</h2>
    <p>The most useful intervention is often operational: put water close to the work, make breaks realistic and train workers and supervisors to recognize heat strain.</p>
    <p>A long cool indoor shift and a shorter day of heavy outdoor labour create very different fluid needs. Plan around workload, heat and sweat, not the clock alone.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Before choosing a drink</p>
  <h2>Four workplace controls</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Potable water</h3><p>Keep safe, cool water near the work area.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Rest and cooling</h3><p>Use shade, ventilation, air conditioning or other appropriate controls when heat is significant.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Acclimatization</h3><p>New and returning workers need gradual exposure to hot environments.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Heat-illness awareness</h3><p>Workers and supervisors should know warning signs and when to stop work.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
